WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:10.840
A beautiful day, flat calm sea and a thousand gannets over your head.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.560
I can't wipe the grin off my face!

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:22.760
The Shetland Islands, as far north as you can get in the British Isles.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.080
A land heaving with wildlife.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:31.640
It's been my dream to get to know Shetland through the seasons of a year with my family.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.240
Have a see!

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:32.720
Thank you.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:38.800
To watch and film the extraordinary, dynamic wildlife.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:46.120
I'm on the trail of some of Britain's rarest birds.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.200
Oh, look, look, look, look, look!

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.080
And the mighty killer whales are back.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:55.200
Shetland has some of the most extraordinary wildlife in Britain.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.960
This is a dream place for me.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.440
My family and I first came to Shetland a year ago.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.280
There's a crab!

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.520
Since then, we've been back here through the seasons...

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:21.120
From the wild winter...

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.040
Whoa, that is deep!

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:26.440
Through the glorious spring.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:31.720
And now it's midsummer and our final stay here.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:40.560
Shetland is the most northerly point in Britain.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:47.240
The archipelago is made up of over 100 islands where you're never more than three miles from the sea.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:55.120
At this time of year, there are millions of wild creatures making the most of the long days to raise their families.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.040
In midsummer you get over 19 hours of sunlight.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:11.919
One of the very best places for seabirds here is Sumburgh Head on the southern tip of the mainland.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:17.760
It's 10 o'clock at night but there's still plenty of light to film by.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:23.960
I've come here with my wife Marguerite, who's also a filmmaker.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:30.120
We're hoping to witness an event which only happens on a few evenings each year in midsummer.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.240
It's something neither one of us has ever seen before.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:43.560
We've come right down to the southernmost point in Shetland to the guillemot colonies here with a view to, I hope, witnessing a really astonishing event.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:48.840
The moment when the chicks leave the nest.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.160
What makes it so astonishing?

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.960
Well, they can't fly when they do it.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.720
They haven't got any feathers, they've just got fluff.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:07.520
And they're going to have to jump from the cliff ledge where they were raised down into the sea below...which seems bonkers!

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:17.880
Guillemots are common in Shetland, but their life story is anything but ordinary.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.840
Each pair raises a single chick in the summer.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:29.800
But whereas most seabird chicks don't leave the nest until they have feathers and can fly properly, young guillemots are just three weeks old and downy when they leave.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:34.240
I love the name they're given.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:39.200
They're called jumplings as opposed to fledglings, which is just perfect.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.640
They haven't got any feathers, so they cane fledglings, so they're a jumpling.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.040
Perfect.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:47.000
But just when they go is unpredictable.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.720
Oh, there's a chick right there.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:49.360
Yeah, really close.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.400
See if I can get it here.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:04:01.560
Until now, these chicks' entire world has been the tiny cliff ledge where they've been raised and fed by Mum and Dad.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.320
But all that's about to end.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:07.840
Over the next couple of weeks, all of them will have to make the jump of their lives...

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:10.800
50 feet down the side of the cliff to the sea below.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:25.240
And to add to their problems, the nest colonies are patrolled by great skuas, bonxies, looking for an easy meal.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:41.520
As the evening wears on, there are fewer predators like bonxies still up and about and that's why the guillemots choose to coax their chicks down to the sea at this late hour.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:46.840
It's gone half past ten at night, but there's still plenty of light.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:47.240
Oh, look!

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:49.160
There's one following its dad.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:50.280
Definitely.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:50.680
Yeah, there he is.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.200
He's got the chick with him.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:56.320
Dad's going to flutter down.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:05:00.000
Very excited-looking father.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.560
It's always the adult male alone that guides the chick to the water.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:12.120
The chick's mother has nothing more to do with her youngster.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.800
A long way down to the sea.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.480
Look at the way the baby hugging its dad.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.920
Sweet...

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.160
Oh, oh, oh!

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:34.080
It looks like the point of no return doesn't it?

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.440
Got nowhere else to slide.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:42.280
There goes Dad.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:47.520
Is he going to go?

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:52.080
Come on, little guy.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.320
He's going to go.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:53.160
He's going to go.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:57.920
Yeah!

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.280
Splosh.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:00.120
Ha, ha!

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:04.120
Sweet.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:11.520
Seeing that chick make the leap of faith, literally the leap of faith, because its father had gone in the water and that's it, no choice now.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:16.920
No going back up the cliff to the nest ledge, because it's not got the wings to do the job.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.360
And bounce like a little cork off the surface of the sea.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.400
And they're away.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:25.200
They're swimming off into the sunset, literally in to the sunset.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:32.360
The very first time this chick has hit the water and it's able to swim like a professional.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:33.520
Other chicks and their fathers are thinking about making a move.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:41.720
As they walk through the colony, they have to deal with neighbours, all defending their tiny patch of cliff ledge from intruders.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:42.320
Every step is fraught with danger.

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:45.320
Another one coming down.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:46.080
Where?

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:48.600
Same place.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:49.000
Where?

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:49.960
I can't see the chick.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:52.160
The chick's below.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:53.160
Has it gone?

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:53.960
It fell.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:56.480
It fell below in front of us, look.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.960
Yes, got it.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.760
I wonder if some ever die falling down.

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:05.160
They're so light I don't think they ever come a cropper by hitting a rock.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.720
But they can fall into a place where they can't escape.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:14.640
And that would mean starvation, so if they fall between two rocks or in a crevice that they can't escape from, then yeah, that would be the end of it.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.040
Oh, slipping.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.400
Ooh...

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:25.160
Look, he's going to go straight.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:26.440
He's definitely going to slip.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:33.760
This chick is desperate to find his way back to its dad.

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:34.840
No wonder the father's looking so anxious.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:38.320
Oh, dear.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:45.680
He's in.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:48.840
Phew!

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:59.760
A brutal and dramatic entry into the marine world for the chick, but this is just the start.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:07.040
Because now, father and chick will paddle all the way to the coast of Norway - some 200 miles away!

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:13.960
It will take them weeks and along the way the chick will grow, learn to fly and feed itself by diving for fish.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:21.400
And just why the Shetland guillemots make this epic journey?

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:31.840
A midsummer's night.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:38.799
In Shetland this time is called the simmer dim.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:44.840
There's nothing else like it in Britain.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:53.520
It's idyllic.

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:57.560
The simmer dim.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:01.760
It's now half past twelve in the middle of the night.

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:12.440
Beautiful red sunset sky.

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:15.600
I don't know whether to call it a sunset or sunrise because it's not going to disappear.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:23.400
The sun is just skimming below the horizon and within a few hours it's going to be back up again.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:29.840
And literally not a breath of wind and I can hear so many different birds.

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:38.680
All of them busy, busy, busy and that's why this place is so rich for life.

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:42.960
That's why there are so many birds nesting here.

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:45.440
They don't run out of the day.

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:58.760
The day continues round the clock and they make the most of this short season to raise their families before the winter closes in and they move off.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:01.760
This is pure, pure magic.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:15.920
A beautiful summer's morning and we're heading for the island of Noss.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:18.560
It makes sense to make it a day out for the whole family.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:24.560
Noss is one of the finest nature reserves in Britain.

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:31.480
Nicknamed Seabird City, home to thousands of our biggest and most dynamic seabirds, gannets.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:36.640
Over the past year we've been following the family fortunes of these birds.

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:41.480
In the winter we fixed two remote cameras near a gannet's nest.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:42.920
Abseiling down a 600-foot cliff to do so.

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:49.880
In the spring, we returned to see if any gannets had nested in view of the cameras.

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:54.840
A pair had taken up residence and they had an egg.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:55.720
Jump in.

00:10:55.080 --> 00:11:05.200
Most of the islands in Shetland are uninhabited, but this wasn't always the case.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:16.880
There are signs of human settlement on Noss that date back to the Bronze Age 4000 years ago and the final settlers left for good in the 1970s.

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:23.520
I hope with the little remote camera, the nest we've been following from the beginning of the year, has got a healthy successful chick on it.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:27.760
Gannets aren't the only birds nesting on Noss.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:36.080
Everything from puffins to skuas breeds here and some, like great black backed gulls, are quick to defend their nests.

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:38.280
OK, here's the gull.

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:42.240
There's a chick just there, hiding in the grass.

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:44.480
You see the babies?

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:47.280
Watch your heads.

00:11:46.400 --> 00:12:01.360
One of the ways they try to dissuade you is to fly very, very close and if you don't move off, they'll use their feet and hit you on the head and Savannah being the highest point, she'll get hit before I do.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:01.960
So we're moving off.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:05.440
Daddy!

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:07.200
Silly, silly birds.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:10.080
They're rather sensible, you know.

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:14.560
Because they're telling us that we're a bit too close to their nest for their comfort.

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:22.640
The trek to the gannet cliffs is about 2 miles.

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:24.680
Uphill most of the way.

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:26.880
At last, we reach our destination.

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:33.240
Sleepy baby.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:35.880
OK, it's just here.

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:38.880
This is where we fixed the remote cameras to the cliffs.

00:12:38.840 --> 00:12:46.920
I have to find the cable ends for the cameras and connect them to a monitor, then we'll see if we have a chick on our nest.

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:49.920
Do you remember where that plug goes?

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:50.360
Mm-hm.

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:50.760
In there?

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:52.240
Can you do that?

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:53.840
Push even harder.

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:54.720
There we go.

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:57.280
Perfect.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:57.840
That's the job.

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:00.760
Right now moment of truth.

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:06.520
One camera was looking down on the nest from above, the other was looking from the side.

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:08.640
I just hope they're still both working.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:10.040
There's nothing there.

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:12.280
Is there anything there?

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:13.120
Oh, oh!

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:16.400
One of the cameras isn't working.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:19.400
Ai-ai-ai-ai!

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:21.560
This is the other one.

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:23.000
This is our last chance, ready, Savannah.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:26.640
What's on there?

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:27.080
Yeah!

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:29.320
We have got a picture.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:30.160
Bit muzzy.

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:31.480
What's that?

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:33.880
That's the gannets.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:36.920
That mummy and a daddy gannet.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:39.240
And we have to see if they've got a chick.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.680
That's the view looking straight down.

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:41.920
Can you see them?

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:42.320
I can see!

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:43.000
Yes there is.

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:44.120
There's a chick underneath her there.

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:44.840
I saw a fluffy edge just there.

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:47.840
Hey, they've got a baby.

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:52.320
They've got a chick and we've got a chick.

00:13:53.440 --> 00:14:03.960
There are about 8000 pairs of gannets on the cliffs here and the sight, sound and smell of this throng of life is breathtaking.

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:09.720
This is looking like a good breeding year.

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:15.480
Lots of nests with chicks, a sign that there's plenty of fish in the sea.

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:17.440
Very promising.

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:17.720
These youngsters have a long way to go.

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:25.480
Unlike the guillemots, gannet chicks stay in the nest until they can fly, and that's at least three months away.

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:28.920
Our pair still has a lot of work to do.

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:32.920
It's super to see that they've got a hatched chick.

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:35.640
Not just a little one either, it's already well underway.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:39.480
Really nice.

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:53.000
Each pair raises just one youngster a year, and it's touching to see the amount of care and dedication lavished by both parent birds on their chick.

00:14:52.160 --> 00:15:05.000
It's astonishing to think that these bundles of fluff will become sleek avian dive bombers, beautifully adapted to plunge diving for fish up to 50 feet below the surface of the water.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:07.480
But the speed with which they hit the water means it's all over in a flash.

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:12.080
I'd love to try and film gannets hunting in slow motion, to reveal what's really going on.

00:15:11.240 --> 00:15:15.360
That's us the long trek back now.

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:16.360
But what a day.

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:20.440
Absolutely gorgeous and great to see that the gannet family have got a youngster.

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:24.440
Nobody fell off a cliff.

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:27.800
And you got us safe and sound.

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:48.560
Shetland attracts wildlife enthusiasts from all over the world, and it's a magnet for scientists too.

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:54.880
Today Marguerite and I are going on a trip with the North Atlantic Killer Whale Research Team.

00:15:54.040 --> 00:16:01.320
They're gathering information about groups of killer whales that travel between Shetland, Iceland and Norway.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:06.840
I've already been lucky enough to encounter some of these killer whales last summer.

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:14.840
First a lone bull, then later a group of males, females and calves hunting seals close to the shore.

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:21.480
Sightings like these are few and far between, so any opportunity to catch up with them is a must.

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:36.200
Just yesterday, the research team had contact with a large group of killer whales a few miles north of the Shetland coast and managed to record some of the remarkable sounds they make, using some rather innovative equipment.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:37.480
Do you know what it is?

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:38.720
It's a wok.

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:39.000
It's fantastic.

00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:42.960
It woks!

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:45.560
It's quite a cool thing.

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:51.160
The team have modified the wok, turning it into a directional underwater microphone.

00:16:50.400 --> 00:17:01.560
Just yesterday, Volker Deecke recorded killer whales communicating with each other using his homemade hydrophone.

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:02.320
This one here.

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:03.600
Wee wop.

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:07.920
And then another one.

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:08.560
Is that the same animal do you think?

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:11.520
They're probably calling back and forth to each other.

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:20.680
Killer whales can sense their prey in a similar way to bats, by using echolocation.

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:25.640
But the team have discovered that they may also use sound in a very different way to hunt.

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:26.920
THUMP Oh, yeah.

00:17:26.079 --> 00:17:29.000
Krr!

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:30.480
Is that the tail?

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:31.200
That's the tail....

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:33.080
Hitting fish.

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:41.160
The idea is, and it's still a working hypothesis, that this very loud call may be uncomfortable to herring.

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:49.200
When herring get uncomfortable, they school together, so this may be a tool to get the herring to school up.

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:53.840
And when they're all in a tight ball, they go whack and hit the whole school with the tail fin.

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:54.480
And then mop up the debilitated fish.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:03.360
With whales having been sighted so recently, there's a strong chance they're still in the neighbourhood.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:05.720
So today we're off on a boat trip to see if we can film them.

00:18:06.680 --> 00:18:15.040
The research team has identified 26 individual killer whales around Shetland.

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:20.760
More seem to be coming into these waters, but they are still very hard to see.

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:22.760
We're going to need a bigger boat.

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:23.320
Yeah.

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:26.760
Most of this is our picnic, you realise.

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:39.240
Our plan today is for the research team to head out on their boat with the listening device, while we travel on a larger boat with local wildlife expert, Brydon Thomason.

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:44.440
Hopefully, between us, we'll pick up the killer whales.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:48.280
The conditions are perfect so I really hope we find them.

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:53.880
We are heading past the very tip of the British Isles.

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:59.960
Muckle Flugga, the lighthouse, the most northerly point anyone has ever lived.

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:03.560
It's not manned any more as a lighthouse.

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:13.920
But Out Stack, that rock just off to the left is the last bit of land before you're out in the open sea.

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:16.600
You go any further north, you're in another country.

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:19.600
But this is where orcas are.

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:29.280
While the research team are using their hydrophone to listen out for the whales, we'll be relying on visual clues.

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:34.240
And, after half an hour's sailing, the signs we were looking for!

00:19:34.160 --> 00:19:41.000
There's a big flock of birds feeding right on our heading now.

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:44.720
Where the gannets are feeding, there are fish.

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:46.240
Where there are fish, there might be killer whales.

00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:47.480
Could be up ahead.

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:54.720
But beneath the birds, it's not the killer whales, it's a close relative of theirs.

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:55.800
Dolphins.

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:56.560
What?

00:19:55.600 --> 00:19:57.440
Dolphins.

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:59.720
Dolphins.

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:04.640
Just coming towards us about 11 o'clock.

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:05.600
Got it, yeah.

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:08.080
Look at that.

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:15.480
White-sided dolphins, hundreds of them!

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:25.160
These are powerful fish hunters of cold north Atlantic waters, and the sea around Shetland is about the best place in Britain to see them.

00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:30.800
They're fast, surging along at almost 30 miles an hour.

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:33.120
There's loads of splashing going on ahead up here.

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:39.320
We've got into a massive feeding group now of seabirds and dolphins.

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:40.960
Big big schools of white-sided dolphins.

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:41.720
The activity is spread right across the horizon.

00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:45.320
There are pockets of birds feeding all over the place.

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:49.400
Big shoals of fish to support this much life.

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:51.800
Who knows...

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:54.480
Oh, beautiful breach just there.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:55.760
That was lovely.

00:20:55.400 --> 00:20:59.120
Great to have found the dolphins!

00:20:58.280 --> 00:21:04.720
But no killer whales today and the research team haven't picked up any sounds either.

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:10.640
The whales will be back though and now we'll be on call with the whale research team.

00:21:09.680 --> 00:21:13.760
I'm hopeful we'll see them before we leave the islands.

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:33.160
To move around Shetland's 15 inhabited islands, you need ferries.

00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:33.600
They are very much part of life up here.

00:21:32.920 --> 00:21:40.560
12 vessels make 190 sailings a day.

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:55.280
I'm off to one of the northern isles to film one of Britain's rarest birds, a creature with rather unconventional breeding habits.

00:21:54.440 --> 00:21:59.920
The red-necked phalarope.

00:21:59.320 --> 00:22:11.480
This is the spot, one of the few places in the British Isles where you can reliably see these gorgeous little birds, red-necked phalaropes.

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:22.800
They're incredibly rare, only about 25 males breed on Shetland so I have to have a special licence to film them.

00:22:22.360 --> 00:22:39.080
Curious, facet of phalarope behaviour, is that they're waders, wading birds like oystercatchers and curlew, but they feed in a way a bit more like a seabird, and only come into these fresh water pools to breed.

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:43.600
I'll just put myself here and see what comes of it.

00:22:45.360 --> 00:22:51.000
Couple of females right there.

00:22:50.040 --> 00:23:00.280
But the most surprising thing about red-necked phalaropes, is that when it comes to raising a family, they reverse roles.

00:22:59.480 --> 00:23:05.920
It's the males that brood the eggs and raise the chicks.

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:08.760
The females simply lay the eggs then clear off.

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:22.680
The female birds on the water in front of me may well have laid eggs in a nest already, but if they get the chance, they may try to get another male to mate with them, and leave him with a family to raise.

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:25.840
Truly liberated females.

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:26.560
(Wow. )

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:27.880
(Look at that. )

00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:33.280
It's like a ballet.

00:23:38.720 --> 00:23:52.280
Amazing to think that right in front of me now, is a good percentage of the British population of red-necked phalarope.

00:23:51.440 --> 00:23:56.480
Oh, there's a male, I think.

00:23:55.720 --> 00:24:03.520
The males are much less brightly coloured than the females.

00:24:03.520 --> 00:24:13.760
He might well have just arrived from his migration and he's clearly worked up an appetite, paddling through the rich soup of tiny flies.

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:18.280
He's not going to get much peace though.

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:22.640
All these females want him to be the father of their eggs.

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:24.840
He's got something this lad.

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:42.000
Girls really having a bit of hair-pulling session, they're all desperately trying to chat him up.

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:46.720
And one female wins over all the rest.

00:24:48.360 --> 00:24:53.600
She could well be the mother of the brood he'll be incubating.

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:57.840
She's effectively defending him against the attentions of other females.

00:24:59.560 --> 00:25:03.840
Hands off girls, he's mine.

00:25:07.520 --> 00:25:15.240
And with the pressure off from the other females, the male can concentrate on getting a meal again.

00:25:15.320 --> 00:25:22.520
Soon the females will leave Shetland and head off to feed on the open sea.

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:26.720
The male's then are left to cope with the eggs on their own.

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:30.480
I'll come back to see how these single fathers manage.

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:34.920
I'm in heaven.

00:25:43.640 --> 00:25:51.360
It's quite probably the finest phalarope evening I have ever had.

00:25:54.640 --> 00:25:59.760
I'm a happy bunny.

00:26:14.600 --> 00:26:31.920
Whether it's the detail in the lives of the wild creatures here, or the sheer scale of the wilderness, Shetland is a place that can suddenly turn your head, and you realise you are in one of the most beautiful places on earth.

00:26:35.200 --> 00:26:48.360
The summer's weather has been exquisite, but the effect of the warm air passing over the cold sea here, causes great banks of mist to roll in from time to time.

00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:57.600
It's put an end to my filming the wildlife for a day or two, but the break gives us the chance to meet Richard Grains.

00:26:56.760 --> 00:27:00.280
I know the weather wasn't looking exactly onside was it?

00:26:59.560 --> 00:27:01.560
No, should be ok though.

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:03.000
What do you think of this?

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:06.360
You're not going to hear the end of the whistle I don't think.

00:27:05.560 --> 00:27:13.680
Richard's a crab fisherman, and he's invited us to come along for his last catch of the day.

00:27:12.880 --> 00:27:22.240
Richard works the creels commercially for velvet swimming crab, lobster, whatever else comes up.

00:27:21.280 --> 00:27:27.280
I just think it's great for Savannah to see part of what makes Shetland tick.

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:33.800
We're heading just off the shore, to where Richard cast his creels a couple of days ago.

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:37.800
Not that you can tell where the land is today!

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:40.760
Where's Shetland?

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:41.480
It's here somewhere.

00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:44.920
We could be in the middle of the North Sea.

00:27:43.960 --> 00:27:45.120
I've no idea.

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:49.640
Richard works single-handed, hauling up the creels or crab traps which have been sitting on the seabed.

00:27:48.800 --> 00:27:51.400
What's that, Savannah?

00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:55.520
Crabs, crabs crabs!

00:27:56.120 --> 00:28:04.520
There's not much market for velvet crabs in the UK, so all of these will be exported live to Spain.

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:08.520
So how many do you normally haul on a day?

00:28:07.560 --> 00:28:13.160
Sometimes 200, sometimes 300.

00:28:12.200 --> 00:28:14.440
Wow!

00:28:13.480 --> 00:28:16.720
200 or 300 creels in a day.

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:18.840
Just think of what that means in terms of physical effort.

00:28:18.080 --> 00:28:24.640
Do you see these little bits at the back, these ones, they're paddles, it uses them for swimming.

00:28:23.720 --> 00:28:26.960
Touch him and see what he feels like.

00:28:26.080 --> 00:28:27.240
What does he feel like?

00:28:26.520 --> 00:28:28.200
Soft.

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:28.760
Soft, exactly!

00:28:27.880 --> 00:28:31.760
Soft and furry, because he's got fluff on his back.

00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:35.720
That's why he's called a velvet swimming crab.

00:28:35.080 --> 00:28:41.080
These are the largest swimming crabs in British waters and are a favourite food of otters.

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:47.440
Bye bye.

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:49.520
But it's not just crabs that find their way into the creels.

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:50.400
Oooh, what's on there?

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:52.280
It's a conger eel!

00:28:51.960 --> 00:28:54.200
No chance.

00:28:53.360 --> 00:28:55.320
Shall I move him out.

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:56.200
I'll put him back.

00:28:55.360 --> 00:28:57.680
Oh, he's gone.

00:28:58.640 --> 00:29:03.720
Look, Savannah, that's a sun star.

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:05.440
Wow, isn't that amazing?

00:29:05.080 --> 00:29:09.120
It's missing one leg.

00:29:08.360 --> 00:29:10.760
Shall I put him back?

00:29:09.960 --> 00:29:12.960
Say, "Bye, bye, sun star."

00:29:12.240 --> 00:29:13.400
Bye bye, sun star.

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:22.840
Richard returns the undersize crabs to the sea before baiting the creels and casting them back in.

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:29.480
He does this every day during the season, in all but the worst storms.

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:41.200
Fortunately the sea mist is short lived and we're back in blazing summer sunshine before long.

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:47.480
Just as well, because our time here is running out and there's so much I still want to see and film.

00:29:46.640 --> 00:29:55.480
One of my big ambitions is to film seabirds with a super slow motion camera, especially gannets plunge diving.

00:29:54.520 --> 00:30:04.480
But before we risk using the camera out at sea, we've come to the island of Mousa to test it out on the arctic tern colony here.

00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:08.520
First, we have to get past the birds' defences, which include dive bombing and pooing on us.

00:30:08.040 --> 00:30:11.360
There's a potential that we're going to get massively attacked by these terns.

00:30:10.640 --> 00:30:15.680
We won't be in the colony, we'll walk around the edge but they're so keen to defend their chicks.

00:30:17.080 --> 00:30:19.400
Ooh, look at him coming.

00:30:19.760 --> 00:30:25.280
We're not even near them yet.

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:27.880
We're also very likely to get shat on.

00:30:27.160 --> 00:30:33.440
Is that English, shat on?

00:30:33.040 --> 00:30:35.080
Shat on, yes.

00:30:34.280 --> 00:30:35.680
Are you sure?

00:30:37.640 --> 00:30:38.840
Yes.

00:30:37.960 --> 00:30:39.960
Or shat upon, actually.

00:30:39.200 --> 00:30:42.200
Shat on.

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:45.320
Now I know what it means.

00:30:44.360 --> 00:30:49.480
It smells of fish, strongly.

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:54.320
We've got the birds, now it's time to get the kit ready.

00:30:53.440 --> 00:30:57.600
There are lots of elements of this set up to get right, so it's worth a last-minute check.

00:30:56.840 --> 00:30:59.120
Marguerite is going to provide the action.

00:30:59.800 --> 00:31:04.080
OK, thank you.

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:04.320
Lovely.

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:06.080
Yeah, you're definitely moving.

00:31:05.240 --> 00:31:10.080
Whee, she can fly!

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:12.320
Wow, look at the lace movement.

00:31:11.680 --> 00:31:15.120
Top lace movement.

00:31:16.160 --> 00:31:17.840
It's nice though because it shows you the slowing down, it's brilliant.

00:31:17.200 --> 00:31:21.560
The camera and computer are working fine.

00:31:20.680 --> 00:31:23.760
Now I just have to plug in the viewfinder and we're ready to go.

00:31:23.640 --> 00:31:26.000
POP!

00:31:28.120 --> 00:31:29.480
Ah!

00:31:29.720 --> 00:31:31.480
Did you hear that?

00:31:30.560 --> 00:31:32.080
Yep.

00:31:32.080 --> 00:31:37.120
That sounded like the viewfinder popping.

00:31:36.600 --> 00:31:38.800
You cannot be serious.

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:44.320
A faulty power lead has blown up the viewfinder.

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:47.560
Try the other battery.

00:31:46.760 --> 00:31:49.680
That is the other battery.

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:50.320
It blew.

00:31:49.520 --> 00:31:51.440
I know it blew.

00:31:50.560 --> 00:31:52.720
I absolutely know it blew.

00:31:53.160 --> 00:31:56.720
Don't you want to try the first one again in case this battery is flat?

00:31:55.760 --> 00:31:56.920
No, I don't.

00:31:56.160 --> 00:31:59.240
Arghhhh!

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:06.760
A broken viewfinder makes it impossible for me to follow the action of the birds in flight.

00:32:05.960 --> 00:32:10.800
All this gear, all this way, for nothing.

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:14.560
I wonder if there is anyone in Shetland who could fix it?

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:24.680
After a few phone calls, I've been directed to a repair shop in Lerwick.

00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:32.200
A power drill and a big screwdriver.

00:32:33.440 --> 00:32:38.480
Not your usual tools for a repair job on a £30,000 camera.

00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:44.560
The person who owns this is going to look back at this and say, "What are they doing to my viewfinder?!"

00:32:44.440 --> 00:33:02.840
Davie and Brian, between them in this Aladdin's cave of electrical wonders, are working hard to try to get a solution so that we can work tomorrow and hopefully, film the gannets or the skuas or anything, for that matter.

00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:04.280
Oh, something came off.

00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:05.200
What is that bit?

00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:09.440
'It's soon obvious to Davie which bit has blown up.'

00:33:08.640 --> 00:33:12.200
If you've got this on the shelf, I'll fall over backwards.

00:33:11.280 --> 00:33:13.520
Any ideas, Brian?

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:17.960
I'd be surprised if we've got anything like that.

00:33:17.200 --> 00:33:21.640
'Brian's had a bit of a rummage and thinks he may have a solution.'

00:33:20.680 --> 00:33:22.480
It should work quite happily.

00:33:23.360 --> 00:33:26.920
It's a different chip but it's doing a similar job.

00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:29.720
If we can make this work, then we might be in business.

00:33:28.920 --> 00:33:32.160
Well, we hope so anyway.

00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:35.480
There's no guarantee this is going to work, mind you.

00:33:34.760 --> 00:33:40.640
That's what we're working towards, making that thing fire up.

00:33:42.040 --> 00:33:43.120
I'll get the other power.

00:33:42.200 --> 00:33:49.640
Last Ditchology, that's where we are!

00:33:49.320 --> 00:33:53.120
"Shetland Scrapstore, Incorporating Last Ditchology."

00:33:52.360 --> 00:33:53.680
That says it all.

00:33:53.320 --> 00:33:55.520
They're magicians, these guys.

00:33:57.600 --> 00:34:03.160
Some things you can fix, some things you canna.

00:34:02.720 --> 00:34:06.160
Listen for the pop-bang.

00:34:05.600 --> 00:34:09.760
Is that smoke coming out there?

00:34:09.320 --> 00:34:12.880
Look, we've got a viewfinder!

00:34:11.920 --> 00:34:14.280
Y-e-e-es!

00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:15.520
Well done!

00:34:14.760 --> 00:34:18.120
Well done!

00:34:17.239 --> 00:34:20.000
How do we put it back together?

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:20.600
Fixed.

00:34:19.719 --> 00:34:21.080
Well done, Brian.

00:34:20.239 --> 00:34:24.719
Now it's fixed we'll need to kill you, because we don't want anyone to know how it's done!

00:34:23.760 --> 00:34:25.639
Cheers, chaps.

00:34:25.719 --> 00:34:28.440
Thanks very, very much indeed.

00:34:28.639 --> 00:34:30.520
Superstars.

00:34:33.280 --> 00:34:37.600
All the stars are lining up for us.

00:34:36.800 --> 00:34:51.440
The weather is stunning again, the slow motion camera AND its viewfinder are working and Tom Jamieson who runs the Mousa Ferry has time to take us on his boat to try film gannets plunge-diving.

00:34:50.560 --> 00:35:00.800
A gannet is never better illustrated its abilities as a hunter of the open seas than when you see it plunging for a fish.

00:35:00.880 --> 00:35:09.040
And to do so 20 or 30 times slower than normal speed, well, that's just going to look magic.

00:35:08.240 --> 00:35:20.240
Gannets range far and wide over the ocean, searching for shoals of fish, but to film them diving very close to the boat, I've brought a few boxes of mackerel.

00:35:19.480 --> 00:35:23.280
And the best place to find gannets in Shetland?

00:35:22.400 --> 00:35:32.400
The seas around Noss, the island where our gannet family is nesting, along with 17,000 neighbours!

00:35:33.040 --> 00:35:37.800
Noss is a stunning, stunning seabird colony.

00:35:36.920 --> 00:35:41.680
This is the cliff where we have our remote camera stationed, looking down at the nest.

00:35:40.720 --> 00:35:42.680
So, our female is sitting on her chick up there somewhere.

00:35:41.880 --> 00:35:46.960
You couldn't pick a better day to admire this great tower block of life.

00:35:46.480 --> 00:35:52.920
The gannets here are used to following the fishing boats and picking off any stray catch.

00:35:51.960 --> 00:35:55.440
They should come quickly to any fish I throw in the sea.

00:35:54.680 --> 00:36:00.800
They're all coming in.

00:35:59.880 --> 00:36:01.040
Good.

00:36:00.160 --> 00:36:06.160
We got the birds, we got the weather, now we just have to have the equipment working...pleeeeeease!

00:36:06.200 --> 00:36:10.280
So, Tom down in front of me here, please.

00:36:13.920 --> 00:36:15.160
Yeah, nice.

00:36:18.200 --> 00:36:21.120
Let's have a look at that.

00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:22.920
Hold it.

00:36:22.040 --> 00:36:27.200
We can instantly view the shots we get on the laptop computer.

00:36:34.120 --> 00:36:39.960
So funky, isn't it, when you see them piling in like that?

00:36:39.280 --> 00:36:40.560
Get ready, Madgy.

00:36:46.440 --> 00:36:50.600
Let's have a look at that.

00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:56.080
The gannets could be hitting the water in excess of 65 mph and they're perfectly adapted for the job.

00:36:55.680 --> 00:36:59.040
They have no external nostrils so the water doesn't shoot up their nose.

00:36:58.080 --> 00:37:12.800
They have special cushions in their foreheads to withstand the impact with the water's surface, and articulated shoulder joints that allow the wings to sweep back along the body, forming the perfect torpedo shape.

00:37:12.400 --> 00:37:24.360
It's such a graphic form and so beautiful, and not only that but water always looks stunning in slow-mo, the splashes and the shine and the silver jewels.

00:37:23.400 --> 00:37:26.000
Everything takes on a whole new atmosphere.

00:37:25.160 --> 00:37:27.920
So yeah, I'm chuffed.

00:37:27.040 --> 00:37:29.240
OK, Let's try another.

00:37:32.280 --> 00:37:35.520
Oh, that was a good one!

00:37:43.080 --> 00:37:55.000
Slowed down by 40 times, it's possible to fully appreciate the flexibility of these high-speed hunters, a sight that is exhilarating and impressive in life is transformed into a beautiful display of precision and grace.

00:38:20.800 --> 00:38:29.840
Tom has to get back to ferry visitors over to the island of Mousa.

00:38:28.960 --> 00:38:34.640
We still have half a box of mackerel left and in my view, the gannets deserve it!

00:38:33.680 --> 00:38:35.440
Look behind you.

00:38:34.560 --> 00:38:37.760
Look at that spectacle now.

00:38:36.800 --> 00:38:39.680
Carpets of gannets following us.

00:38:38.720 --> 00:38:40.400
Look.

00:38:39.640 --> 00:38:46.920
I feel like the Pied Piper of Shetland!

00:38:53.480 --> 00:39:04.240
Mousa, the uninhabited island Tom runs the ferry to, is a nature reserve with some very special inhabitants - storm petrels.

00:39:03.480 --> 00:39:18.280
They're tiny seabirds about the size of a sparrow and as such, they're very vulnerable to attack from daytime hunters, like gulls and skuas so they only fly in to land under the cover of darkness.

00:39:17.720 --> 00:39:24.080
The best way for us to film them is to stay on the island, and that will mean camping with the whole family.

00:39:23.800 --> 00:39:28.160
Unfortunately, though, conditions have taken a turn for the worse.

00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:28.960
Wow.

00:39:28.080 --> 00:39:32.200
Not so sure about this swell.

00:39:31.280 --> 00:39:34.840
Tom, the skipper of the boat, is on his way.

00:39:34.200 --> 00:39:41.280
I can see quite a lot of white water breaking off the front of the boat but if he says it's OK, then it's OK.

00:39:41.560 --> 00:39:43.800
Hi, Tom.

00:39:43.040 --> 00:39:45.160
Swelly old day.

00:39:44.280 --> 00:39:45.960
How are you?

00:39:45.440 --> 00:39:48.640
I'm fine, thanks.

00:39:47.760 --> 00:39:50.360
How do you rate it for the other side?

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:50.960
Oh, no problem there.

00:39:50.160 --> 00:39:53.000
It's a lovely day at Mousa.

00:39:52.520 --> 00:39:53.600
Yeah?

00:39:52.920 --> 00:39:54.320
Yes.

00:39:53.400 --> 00:39:56.480
No, I'm not joking.

00:39:57.240 --> 00:40:04.080
It's only 1.5 miles away, how different can it be?

00:40:03.240 --> 00:40:09.640
Even though there's swell, there's not much wind bringing it on, he says.

00:40:08.920 --> 00:40:11.240
I like the bouncy thing!

00:40:10.640 --> 00:40:13.840
That was a big wave.

00:40:14.880 --> 00:40:21.240
Of course, Tom was right - out at Mousa, it's flat calm.

00:40:21.200 --> 00:40:24.880
Let's run up here.

00:40:24.040 --> 00:40:30.480
The tower on the island is in fact an ancient fort called a broch.

00:40:29.600 --> 00:40:33.240
It's over 2,000 years old.

00:40:33.480 --> 00:40:39.160
Now it offers safety and sanctuary for nesting storm petrels.

00:40:38.280 --> 00:40:41.080
We won't see them until it gets dark and that gives us time to organise our own accommodation.

00:40:40.280 --> 00:40:46.600
This is the first time Savannah has been camping, spending a whole night inside a tent.

00:40:45.720 --> 00:40:46.920
There we go.

00:40:46.080 --> 00:40:47.520
That was quick and easy, isn't it?

00:40:46.680 --> 00:40:50.320
Mummy, soup is ready!

00:40:50.120 --> 00:40:55.240
After dinner, it's time to investigate the tower.

00:40:55.560 --> 00:40:57.400
What's that?

00:40:56.640 --> 00:40:59.200
Castle.

00:40:58.440 --> 00:41:00.600
Who lives in the castle?

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:01.840
Princess.

00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:02.960
Are you a princess?

00:41:02.120 --> 00:41:04.080
Yeah.

00:41:07.440 --> 00:41:08.560
OK.

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:20.000
The light is fading, the clouds are gathering and the storm petrels will soon start to call to their mates to guide them in from the sea.

00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:26.960
Once they're on land, they're very clumsy so they wait for the darkest nights before risking a visit to their breeding sites.

00:41:26.320 --> 00:41:35.360
Some people call them storm petrels but we can call them night faeries and they sing to each other.

00:41:34.680 --> 00:41:36.200
It's dark.

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:39.200
It's sleepy time.

00:41:38.480 --> 00:41:44.320
This is where the night faeries live.

00:41:43.520 --> 00:41:47.400
But they're not talking yet.

00:41:46.480 --> 00:41:47.800
Come in with me.

00:41:46.960 --> 00:41:49.200
Hold my hand.

00:41:48.440 --> 00:41:50.920
I can see in here.

00:41:50.200 --> 00:41:52.920
Look in there, in that hole.

00:41:52.200 --> 00:41:56.560
There'll be a little bird that lives down at the end there, somewhere.

00:41:55.640 --> 00:42:00.560
It's called a storm petrel.

00:42:00.480 --> 00:42:06.120
The petrels can recognise the voice of their mate who's sitting in the nest.

00:42:05.440 --> 00:42:13.120
That's what we're beginning to hear - the voices of the brooding birds, calling their partners so they can swap incubation duties.

00:42:12.320 --> 00:42:18.600
One is starting to talk.

00:42:17.680 --> 00:42:23.160
Can you here it going "neh-neh"?

00:42:23.320 --> 00:42:26.240
What are those?

00:42:25.960 --> 00:42:28.760
(That's the night faeries. )

00:42:28.120 --> 00:42:32.600
(The storm petrels.

00:42:31.720 --> 00:42:33.400
They say "neh-neh.")

00:42:32.440 --> 00:42:36.480
(Then they start singing. )

00:42:35.520 --> 00:42:37.080
(They sound like they're giggling. )

00:42:36.200 --> 00:42:43.240
(Little night faeries laughing. )

00:42:42.480 --> 00:42:53.240
Both the male and female take turns to incubate the egg while their partner heads out to sea to feed, often for two or three days at a time.

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:55.080
They nest in the walls around here.

00:42:54.600 --> 00:43:03.440
They nest on the beach but there's something extra-special about being inside the broch with its history and its mystery and these little voices from the ocean singing out from the walls.

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:09.200
I love it.

00:43:08.320 --> 00:43:15.200
They live in this castle and they dance and they dance.

00:43:14.520 --> 00:43:15.800
They do.

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:18.800
They dance in the air.

00:43:18.080 --> 00:43:21.360
(You can hear them giggling!)

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:47.320
It's been almost three weeks since I filmed the rare red-necked phalaropes on the northern isles.

00:43:46.440 --> 00:43:53.400
Now I want to return to see how those single fathers are getting on.

00:43:52.520 --> 00:43:56.680
The females are probably long gone, and the males will be on their own with the eggs.

00:43:56.120 --> 00:44:07.160
Marguerite and I are being helped by RSPB wardens Malkie Smith and his wife, Martha.

00:44:06.240 --> 00:44:07.520
OK.

00:44:06.680 --> 00:44:09.720
You lead the way.

00:44:08.920 --> 00:44:16.760
Martha and Malkie monitor all the nests they can find of these incredibly rare birds to help with their conservation.

00:44:17.280 --> 00:44:25.120
There are fewer than 25 males nesting here, so we've been granted special licence to film them at the nest.

00:44:24.440 --> 00:44:30.480
Their precious eggs and the young chicks are incredibly well-hidden and could easily be crushed by a careless boot.

00:44:29.680 --> 00:44:32.200
Just walk in the low patches again and try not to go on the tussocks.

00:44:34.400 --> 00:44:38.800
The chicks could be on this marshy bit.

00:44:38.560 --> 00:44:41.680
I'll walk in your footsteps.

00:44:41.240 --> 00:44:47.520
But, fortunately, with Malkie as our guide, we know precisely where to go.

00:44:46.760 --> 00:44:49.960
The nest is beautifully camouflaged, invisible.

00:44:50.680 --> 00:44:58.720
With the male away feeding, we can get prepared for filming.

00:44:57.920 --> 00:45:04.360
There's four eggs, they look like they're almost hatching.

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:07.040
I think we should set it up with the lipstick camera.

00:45:06.560 --> 00:45:12.120
I'm going to use one of my mini-cams to film the nest.

00:45:11.400 --> 00:45:12.480
But I don't want it to stand out like a sore thumb.

00:45:11.640 --> 00:45:15.800
A bit of improvised camouflage, and we're in business.

00:45:14.960 --> 00:45:18.920
Phalarope cam!

00:45:17.960 --> 00:45:25.520
Now I just have to position the camera by the nest and do my best to hide it.

00:45:24.760 --> 00:45:34.960
Working with a nest like this is always a race against time, because you don't want to keep the birds away from the nest more than a few minutes.

00:45:34.200 --> 00:45:36.360
Almost ready.

00:45:35.960 --> 00:45:46.120
Malkie and Martha have headed off to check other nests, and all we have to do is get the gear and ourselves into the hide - easier said than done.

00:45:45.280 --> 00:45:47.440
Very cosy.

00:45:46.760 --> 00:45:51.400
Got nowhere to put my leg.

00:45:51.160 --> 00:45:54.080
He's back, he's back.

00:45:53.120 --> 00:45:54.320
He's coming.

00:46:11.360 --> 00:46:12.720
He's going straight in.

00:46:11.880 --> 00:46:13.360
Go on, go on, go on.

00:46:12.400 --> 00:46:13.680
Yes, he's on.

00:46:15.360 --> 00:46:19.520
Beautifully camouflaged, that nest.

00:46:18.800 --> 00:46:21.680
When he goes inside, you can't see him at all from here.

00:46:24.760 --> 00:46:33.600
Those rings on his legs, those are colour rings that Malkie has been putting on the birds for years.

00:46:32.720 --> 00:46:37.960
And it means he can identify them as individuals.

00:46:37.520 --> 00:46:41.120
He's really busy on the housework!

00:46:43.040 --> 00:46:47.240
What's Mum up to?

00:46:46.280 --> 00:46:47.800
She's off with the girls.

00:46:46.880 --> 00:46:53.160
Party, party, party maybe even with another bloke.

00:46:55.160 --> 00:47:04.560
She may well lay eggs in another nest, and have another male tend another brood.

00:47:03.680 --> 00:47:09.720
After about 40 minutes incubating the eggs, he's off again.

00:47:10.160 --> 00:47:18.520
Being a single parent, he's got no-one to look after the eggs when he needs a meal, and he's got to do that at least once every hour.

00:47:17.680 --> 00:47:19.720
Do you know?

00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:24.240
I can't bear watching a nest without the adult on it.

00:47:23.520 --> 00:47:24.840
Can you?

00:47:24.080 --> 00:47:27.840
Because it's... just so vulnerable.

00:47:27.000 --> 00:47:28.240
'An hour later...'

00:47:27.400 --> 00:47:28.960
It's not funny anymore.

00:47:37.720 --> 00:47:39.440
What's happened to him?

00:47:40.720 --> 00:47:43.760
It's getting colder as well, there's a mist coming in.

00:47:43.920 --> 00:47:49.000
Concerned, I call Malkie for advice.

00:47:48.320 --> 00:47:50.520
He just went off and hasn't been back.

00:47:51.600 --> 00:47:57.160
But Malkie has some very reassuring news.

00:47:56.640 --> 00:47:58.880
Well I'm glad you know where he is.

00:47:57.920 --> 00:48:01.760
He's with Malkie, at a different...

00:48:01.520 --> 00:48:04.040
Where Malkie's doing the ringing.

00:48:03.360 --> 00:48:04.520
Amazing.

00:48:03.760 --> 00:48:05.000
Thank you.

00:48:04.040 --> 00:48:07.520
He'll let me know when he's coming!

00:48:08.920 --> 00:48:10.000
OK, Bye.

00:48:11.280 --> 00:48:15.440
Malkie's ringing phalaropes at a different...

00:48:14.680 --> 00:48:18.640
It's about 3.5 - 4 km.

00:48:17.680 --> 00:48:20.440
And our male just turned up.

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:23.720
Two white rings and a BTO ring.

00:48:23.000 --> 00:48:24.120
Cup of tea then?

00:48:23.960 --> 00:48:28.280
That's what he just said, "Have a cup of tea because I can see your male".

00:48:27.480 --> 00:48:30.120
Cup of tea time.

00:48:30.360 --> 00:48:33.520
I feel a bit better now.

00:48:32.720 --> 00:48:37.280
After what turns out to be two or three cups of tea, our wanderer returns.

00:48:38.400 --> 00:48:41.120
It's great to see him back.

00:48:41.440 --> 00:48:42.720
I can now breathe again.

00:48:48.000 --> 00:48:53.800
And heave an enormous sigh of relief.

00:48:57.040 --> 00:48:59.840
Because for a while there, I thought maybe he'd been nobbled by a predator.

00:49:21.080 --> 00:49:29.920
With this being our last trip to Shetland, I can't leave without spending some time with one of my favourite animals here.

00:49:29.080 --> 00:49:42.160
Together with my friend and local naturalist, John Campbell, I've been following a family of otters over the past few months on a remote stretch of coastline in the east.

00:49:41.480 --> 00:49:48.440
The last time I saw them, the mother was desperately trying to get her well-grown cubs to leave home and start life on their own.

00:49:47.680 --> 00:49:53.400
I want to find out how they're getting on, and it's not long before we spot the male cub fishing in the lagoon.

00:49:52.880 --> 00:49:54.000
He's one of three cubs.

00:49:53.160 --> 00:49:59.600
He's got two sisters, who don't seem to be around at the moment.

00:49:58.720 --> 00:50:01.520
Beautiful to have him coming so close.

00:50:00.680 --> 00:50:03.800
He's going straight across to the other shore.

00:50:02.920 --> 00:50:04.200
I think he's going to come out.

00:50:11.080 --> 00:50:15.320
He's calling, and that means he still wants to be with his mother.

00:50:14.480 --> 00:50:17.680
No sign of her though.

00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:21.680
Ah, there she is!

00:50:21.200 --> 00:50:23.000
They're still together.

00:50:25.080 --> 00:50:27.640
But she doesn't look happy about it.

00:50:32.360 --> 00:50:37.560
He's so needy, this young male.

00:50:36.600 --> 00:50:41.840
He must be 14 months old and she doesn't want him with her.

00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:43.680
Look at her, turning on him.

00:50:42.760 --> 00:50:47.760
This is the mother telling the cub to keep back.

00:50:48.720 --> 00:50:52.800
All she wants to do is get away from him.

00:50:51.920 --> 00:50:56.160
"Don't follow me, son, "You're on your own now."

00:50:55.800 --> 00:51:01.720
That's what the whickering calling is all about.

00:51:01.320 --> 00:51:09.120
She's got other things on her mind and he's just holding on to his mum's apron strings, saying, "Come on Mum, can I have a cuddle?".

00:51:08.280 --> 00:51:09.480
And she's saying, "No.

00:51:08.520 --> 00:51:11.240
You're a big lad".

00:51:10.320 --> 00:51:15.520
He can look after himself, feed himself.

00:51:15.200 --> 00:51:16.640
He just wants the company.

00:51:16.840 --> 00:51:19.560
She's gone down into the holt.

00:51:18.760 --> 00:51:21.760
He doesn't know quite where to put himself.

00:51:23.920 --> 00:51:27.200
He's just calling his heart out.

00:51:36.840 --> 00:51:41.560
Sounds like there another one over here.

00:51:41.080 --> 00:51:42.720
It's another two.

00:51:41.920 --> 00:51:44.280
It's his sisters.

00:51:43.440 --> 00:51:45.360
That's all three cubs.

00:51:47.000 --> 00:51:49.440
And Mum still hiding in the holt.

00:51:51.640 --> 00:51:54.920
Going to look inside, going in the front door.

00:51:55.800 --> 00:52:01.160
Oh, Mum is telling them all to get lost.

00:52:00.200 --> 00:52:07.200
They're desperate to try and get back into the company of their mother.

00:52:06.320 --> 00:52:07.840
She's having none of it.

00:52:06.880 --> 00:52:09.280
They just can't leave her alone.

00:52:08.840 --> 00:52:12.080
But here she comes, that's Mum coming out.

00:52:11.280 --> 00:52:12.640
What's she going to do?

00:52:14.040 --> 00:52:16.680
Oh and she's actually going to groom them.

00:52:16.640 --> 00:52:23.120
She's finally relented and thought, "OK, kids, if you must".

00:52:24.480 --> 00:52:33.720
This period of separation is so difficult for the cubs, but it's essential too, if they're to lead an independent life.

00:52:33.160 --> 00:52:37.000
Very sweet, right, let's catch up.

00:52:39.920 --> 00:52:44.120
They've all gone out to the sea.

00:52:46.520 --> 00:52:52.600
After half an hour, we find one.

00:52:51.640 --> 00:52:56.120
It's the mother.

00:52:55.240 --> 00:52:57.240
There's no sign of the cubs.

00:52:56.440 --> 00:53:00.320
She's managed to give them the slip.

00:53:00.640 --> 00:53:06.960
This could well be the first time she's been alone for over a year.

00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:12.160
No wonder she's keeping a low profile.

00:53:13.600 --> 00:53:21.360
It will be another three months at least before she's caring for a new family.

00:53:23.120 --> 00:53:26.800
Until then she'll be hunting for herself, alone.

00:53:30.160 --> 00:53:35.320
And can enjoy deep, peaceful sleep.

00:53:42.200 --> 00:53:47.360
Our time here on Shetland is coming to a close.

00:53:46.680 --> 00:53:52.520
We've had an awesome year, getting a flavour of the place in all its moods.

00:53:51.720 --> 00:53:55.200
And the wildlife has been first class.

00:53:54.800 --> 00:53:59.680
But Shetland has one last natural treasure in store for us.

00:53:58.840 --> 00:54:07.360
We've just had a call from Helen Moncrieff, the warden down at Sumburgh.

00:54:06.480 --> 00:54:11.840
She said, orcas have just been seen off the point and heading west, on the other side of the island.

00:54:11.880 --> 00:54:15.440
So who knows if we're going to see them, but it's definitely worth a go.

00:54:14.520 --> 00:54:18.280
We need to get moving to see if we can catch up with them.

00:54:17.520 --> 00:54:21.800
We're enlisting the help of Tom, the Mousa ferryman, again.

00:54:20.920 --> 00:54:24.840
He's got a couple of hours before his next scheduled crossing.

00:54:24.000 --> 00:54:26.360
Suddenly, more good news.

00:54:25.440 --> 00:54:27.520
What's happened?

00:54:26.680 --> 00:54:28.960
They've been spotted nearby.

00:54:28.240 --> 00:54:30.600
They're off the south end of Mousa.

00:54:29.880 --> 00:54:32.160
Excellent!

00:54:31.400 --> 00:54:37.520
The research team can move much faster than we can in their smaller boat.

00:54:37.200 --> 00:54:41.360
We just have to hope the killers come our way.

00:54:40.480 --> 00:54:46.440
I think they're just off Noness, which is just around this headland.

00:54:45.560 --> 00:54:47.240
Noness?

00:54:46.400 --> 00:54:49.800
Apparently they're just off Noness.

00:54:48.960 --> 00:54:52.920
Obviously watch your time for your pick-up, as well.

00:54:52.040 --> 00:54:54.200
There's loads of time, it's only 3.30pm.

00:54:54.160 --> 00:54:59.920
The number of killer whales that come close to shore is very small.

00:54:59.280 --> 00:55:05.480
Groups of five or six are the most you might see, and then only if you're very lucky.

00:55:04.640 --> 00:55:07.280
No sign just yet.

00:55:06.440 --> 00:55:09.280
What's that?

00:55:08.520 --> 00:55:10.720
Is that the rib there?

00:55:09.880 --> 00:55:13.800
That's the research team.

00:55:13.880 --> 00:55:15.080
We must be getting close.

00:55:14.120 --> 00:55:15.400
There.

00:55:14.640 --> 00:55:17.880
We got orcas coming towards us right now.

00:55:16.920 --> 00:55:20.920
What we'll do is get a little bit closer and switch the engines off.

00:55:20.080 --> 00:55:22.440
Oh, look, look, look!

00:55:21.560 --> 00:55:27.280
We've got a bull right here.

00:55:28.480 --> 00:55:31.040
That's the youngsters.

00:55:30.600 --> 00:55:35.520
Look, a big group, with the male there!

00:55:34.600 --> 00:55:36.440
We've got a gang coming in here.

00:55:37.240 --> 00:55:39.160
Here they come.

00:55:38.600 --> 00:55:40.120
They're coming off the stern.

00:55:39.440 --> 00:55:41.600
They're heading this way, folks.

00:55:41.680 --> 00:55:42.720
Look, here we go!

00:55:42.880 --> 00:55:43.960
Right here.

00:55:45.720 --> 00:55:49.640
That's the sound of their breath.

00:55:49.240 --> 00:55:54.600
They're just 30 feet away.

00:55:53.800 --> 00:55:57.960
How beautiful is that?

00:55:57.200 --> 00:56:01.720
This is unlike anything we've ever seen before.

00:56:00.960 --> 00:56:05.600
There are killer whales all around us.

00:56:06.280 --> 00:56:07.640
At least 15, maybe more!

00:56:19.720 --> 00:56:27.280
What's extraordinary here is we've got orcas all over the place.

00:56:26.440 --> 00:56:30.520
We seem to be surrounded by them.

00:56:29.680 --> 00:56:37.520
It looks like several different families are all meeting and greeting.

00:56:37.400 --> 00:56:43.120
It's 4.35pm, and within an hour.

00:56:42.200 --> 00:56:46.680
We've seen at least 20 orcas.

00:56:45.800 --> 00:56:55.240
We now have the opportunity to get on land and see if we can catch up with the orcas from the land.

00:56:54.400 --> 00:56:59.120
I think they're going to head off hunting now.

00:56:58.320 --> 00:56:59.640
They looked like they were moving inshore.

00:56:59.640 --> 00:57:09.520
We've travelled down to Sumburgh Head the most southerly point of Shetland's mainland, and quite a crowd has already gathered for this wildlife spectacle.

00:57:08.680 --> 00:57:10.640
Beautiful.

00:57:09.840 --> 00:57:13.160
Lovely, lovely light.

00:57:14.240 --> 00:57:21.720
Most of the killer whales have spread out, back into the deeper waters.

00:57:21.240 --> 00:57:25.600
But a few are still very close.

00:57:25.200 --> 00:57:31.920
The end of a perfect day, the killer whales just offshore.

00:57:31.040 --> 00:57:38.040
For me, it's one last contact with the island's biggest and most impressive predators.

00:57:37.360 --> 00:57:39.840
That's it, they're off.

00:57:39.080 --> 00:57:42.280
Personally, I'm very content.

00:57:41.520 --> 00:57:46.880
Spending time with these magnificent animals and seeing so many is marvellous.

00:57:46.400 --> 00:57:48.280
Being close to them...

00:57:48.960 --> 00:57:57.840
Very fulfilling, and on a day like this - calm, blue skies, warm - it doesn't get much better.

00:57:59.240 --> 00:58:06.800
Shetland has offered us her most precious natural treasures over the year.

00:58:06.080 --> 00:58:12.760
And the passion that brought me and my family here in the first place has only grown deeper.

00:58:11.960 --> 00:58:14.880
We have to leave now.

00:58:14.120 --> 00:58:18.560
Our journey on these northern isles is over.

00:58:17.720 --> 00:58:22.520
But their wild character, space and above all beauty, will remain with us, forever.
